# SD Core for Linux - the privileged helper.
#
# Written by installsdai.sh to /etc/sudoers.d/sdcore, mode 0440, root:root.
# Removed by deletesdai.sh.  Validate with:
#
#     visudo -cf /etc/sudoers.d/sdcore
#
# WHY THIS NAMES ONE COMMAND AND NOT EIGHT.
#
# Ten call sites in GPL.BP shell out to sudo: useradd, userdel, usermod,
# groupadd, groupdel, deluser, passwd and chmod g+s.  Naming those here would
# grant root by another route, because not one of them is restricted by its
# arguments - "sudo passwd root", "sudo usermod -aG sudo <me>" and
# "sudo chmod g+s <anything>" would all be permitted.
#
# So sudoers names only sd-elevate, and sd-elevate decides what may happen.
# It whitelists SD's own groups (sdusers, sdu_*, sdg_*), refuses root, sdsys
# and every account below UID_MIN, and confines chmod g+s to the accounts
# root.  gplbld/test-sd-elevate.py drives those rules.
#
# A command listed with no arguments permits ANY arguments - that is
# deliberate here, because the helper is what validates them.
#
# TWO PROPERTIES THIS ENTRY DEPENDS ON, both enforced by the installer:
#
#   1. /usr/local/sbin/sd-elevate is root-owned and NOT writable by sdsys or
#      sdusers.  It lives outside /usr/local/sdsys precisely because that tree
#      is chown -R sdsys:sdusers'd - a helper inside it could be rewritten by
#      anyone who reached the sdsys account, and this entry would then hand
#      them root.
#   2. /etc/sudoers has its "#includedir /etc/sudoers.d" line, or this file is
#      never read and the account verbs fail with no explanation.

%sdadmin ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/sbin/sd-elevate
